{
  "protocol": "snmp",
  "title": "SNMPv2c",
  "source": "RFC 3416 (operations), RFC 3417 (transport), X.690 (BER)",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "snmp",
      "kind": "protocol",
      "about": [
        "SNMPv2c — RFC 3416 (the operations), RFC 3417 (over UDP 161), X.690 (how it is written).",
        "",
        "Everything here is BER: a tag octet, a length, and that many octets, nested. Which makes this",
        "the first description whose lengths are not a width the author chose. A BER length is ONE octet",
        "when what follows is under 128 and 0x80+n followed by n octets when it is not, so how wide a",
        "length field is depends on the number it holds, which depends on how wide the lengths inside it",
        "are. The request's five nested lengths are one octet each; the response's are 3, 3, 3, 3 and 2,",
        "and the difference is one string being 247 octets long.",
        "",
        "That is not a cycle, and the reason it is not is worth stating: a length measures a span, and a",
        "span is as wide as its members whatever offset it lands at. So it settles bottom-up — the",
        "innermost value is known, so its length's width is known, so the span holding both is known. The",
        "engine does this by asking rather than by passing: a set's extent waits on its members' extents,",
        "and a variable-width field's extent waits on its value.",
        "",
        "Every length below is written the same way — `escaped`, limit 128, bound four octets, which is",
        "X.690 §8.1.3 exactly. Under 128 the octet IS the length; at 128 and above it is 0x80 plus a",
        "count of the octets that follow, and those hold the number minimally. Four is a bound rather",
        "than a rule: §8.1.3.5 allows 126 length octets, and a datagram claiming its next span runs to",
        "2^1000 octets is not one worth reading on to find out.",
        "",
        "What SNMP does NOT have, and this is the finding rather than an omission: a discriminated union.",
        "GetRequest and GetResponse differ in one octet and in nothing else. A varbind's value is a tag, a",
        "length and that many octets whatever the type is — NULL is length nought, and TimeTicks is four",
        "octets that only a MIB knows to read as hundredths. So the tag is carried and checked, and what",
        "the octets MEAN is the caller's, exactly as a BACnet Unsigned is. A description that forked on the",
        "value tag would refuse every type it had not enumerated, and SNMP's whole point is that new ones",
        "arrive without the message format changing.",
        "",
        "Scope, stated in the octets rather than here: version is 1 and is checked, so v1 and v3 are",
        "refused; the PDU tag admits GetRequest and GetResponse and refuses GetNext, GetBulk, Set, Trap",
        "and Inform, each of which is a different operation with its own rules about what the two",
        "error fields mean."
      ]
    },
