    {
      "id": "moreArcs",
      "kind": "junction",
      "as": "another arc?",
      "about": [
        "A name carries no arc count and no terminator, and an arc that ends is followed by another that",
        "begins the same way. What says where it stops is the OID's own length — so the reading asks",
        "where it is against where that length runs out.",
        "",
        "Which is a bounded region in the middle of a message rather than at the end of it: what follows",
        "the last arc is this binding's value, and the reading has to stop at exactly the right octet or",
        "read a tag as an arc. The bound is worked out from three facts about ONE field — where the",
        "length octet sat, how wide it turned out to be, and what it said — because those are the three",
        "things that fix where its span ends."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.moreArcs",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "is there another arc?",
      "runs": "position < at + wide + held",
      "gives": "Bool",
      "takes": {
        "at": "Int",
        "wide": "Int",
        "held": "Int"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "moreVarbinds",
      "kind": "junction",
      "as": "another binding?",
      "about": [
        "The same question one level out, against the varbind list's length — and the same answer,",
        "which is the point of asking it this way rather than 'is there anything left'.",
        "",
        "There would be a cheaper test here: the varbind list is the last thing in the PDU which is the",
        "last thing in the message, so nothing at all follows it and 'are there octets left' would do.",
        "It is not written that way because it would be true of this protocol by accident — the moment",
        "anything is appended after the list, a reading that stopped at the end of the datagram would",
        "swallow it, and nothing in the description would say why."
      ]
    },
